 # Uniform Ray Sampling
 
-Also called: Stratified Sampling along Rays, Uniform Point Sampling, Uniform Point Sampling along Rays, Uniform Sampling, Uniform Sampling with Noise
+Also called: Uniform Sampling, Uniform Point Sampling, Uniform Point Sampling along Rays, Uniform Distance Sampling, Regular Sampling
 
 ## Overview
-Uniform Ray Sampling generates sample points along camera rays by distributing depths uniformly between a near clipping plane ($t_n$) and a far clipping plane ($t_f$) [S2][S5, S7, unofficial]. It assumes that every point along the ray contributes equally to the final rendering, implying a constant probability density function (PDF) and a linearly decaying transmittance [S2]. This method serves as the baseline for NeRF-style raymarching and is the simplest sampling strategy to implement, though it typically offers the lowest efficiency compared to spatial skipping or importance sampling methods because it does not adapt to scene geometry or density variations [S2].
+Uniform Ray Sampling generates sample points along camera rays by distributing depths uniformly between a near clipping plane ($t_n$) and a far clipping plane ($t_f$) [S1, S6, unofficial][S7][S8, S9, S13, S14, unofficial][S16, book][S18, unofficial]. It assumes that every point along the ray contributes equally to the final rendering, implying a constant probability density function (PDF) and a linearly decaying transmittance [S3]. This method serves as the baseline for NeRF-style raymarching and is the simplest sampling strategy to implement, though it typically offers the lowest efficiency compared to spatial skipping or importance sampling methods because it does not adapt to scene geometry or density variations [S3].
 
